From 0fe0ff1f224ed374e99de0340f68c3ac10f743a1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: RoninSwap Dev <dev@roninswap.local>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 11:23:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] fix(wallet-link): per-wallet consumption ledger prevents
 unlink/relink double-claim
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

ORIGINAL BUG
-----------
The first migration used wallets.claimed_points on the canonical Solana
wallet as the authoritative 'consumed points' counter for the entire
verified identity. This created an accounting bug under the
link → claim → unlink → earn → relink → claim cycle:

  Solana A: 25 SP, EVM: 150 SP → identity 175 SP
  Claim 175 SP → Solana A.claimed_points = 175
  Unlink EVM → EVM's 150 SP becomes 'fresh' from Solana A's perspective
  Re-link EVM (to Solana A OR to a different Solana B) → the EVM's
    150 SP becomes claimable AGAIN because consumed_points still
    lives on Solana's row, not on the EVM's row.

THE FIX
-------
Add a new public.wallet_point_consumption table that records per-wallet
consumption. Each row ties a wallet_id to a claim_id with a consumed
amount. The consumed amount TRAVELS WITH THE WALLET_ID, not with the
link, so unlink/relink cannot restore consumed points.

Math:
  earned_points(wallet)   = sum(samurai_points.final_points)
  consumed_points(wallet)  = sum(wallet_point_consumption.points_consumed)
  claimable(identity)      = sum(earned across identity)
                            - sum(consumed across identity)

FIFO DISTRIBUTION
-----------------
When a claim is made, consumed points are distributed FIFO across the
identity wallets:
  1. Canonical Solana wallet (oldest by definition)
  2. Linked EVM wallets ordered by verified_at ASC
For each wallet: take min(remaining, max(earned - consumed, 0)) and
insert a wallet_point_consumption row. If distribution can't account
for the full amount, raise CONSUMPTION_DISTRIBUTION_FAILED (defensive
— should never happen because we validated points_to_claim ≤ claimable
upstream).

BACKWARD COMPATIBILITY
---------------------
- wallets.claimed_points column is PRESERVED (not dropped, not reset).
  It's kept in sync on new claims (incremented on canonical Solana)
  for legacy admin tooling, but it is NOT the authoritative source.
- Existing users with claimed_points > 0 are BACKFILLED via a single
  MIGRATION_BACKFILL row in wallet_point_consumption per wallet. This
  makes the new accounting consistent from the moment the migration is
  applied — existing users cannot reclaim their previously-claimed
  points.
- The backfill is IDEMPOTENT: a partial unique index on
  (wallet_id) WHERE source='MIGRATION_BACKFILL' makes the migration
  safe to re-run.

EVOLUTION OF THE LINK TABLE
---------------------------
Removed evm_chain_scope from wallet_links. An EVM address is one row
in public.wallets regardless of which EVM chain it swapped on
(chain_id lives on swap_transactions / samurai_points, not on wallets).
A per-chain link scope would split a single EVM identity into multiple
pseudo-identities without any security benefit. The link is between
two wallet addresses, period.

WHAT'S UNCHANGED
----------------
- Solana Anchor rewards program 6Uyjo8oDGQJeb8zS1yFqwLCguc4gfUB1V4xWheAD7RYC
- Reward PDA seeds, vault, claim_reward instruction
- Existing reward_claims rows (no migration touches them)
- Existing wallets.claimed_points values (only incremented going forward)
- Existing samurai_points rows
- The dual-sig (EIP-191 + ed25519) link security model
- The unique partial index preventing cross-user EVM hijack
- The one-time-use challenge lifecycle (PENDING → USED)

NEW TESTS
---------
- scripts/test_wallet_link_accounting_scenarios.py — Python simulation
  of all 5 scenarios (S1 normal link, S2 legacy Solana-only, S3 partial
  claims, S4 unlink after claim, S5 unlink-earn-relink) + cross-identity
  hijack accounting test. 6 tests, all pass.
- scripts/test_wallet_link_accounting_scenarios.sql — Live SQL test
  script that runs the same scenarios against a real Postgres/Supabase
  instance. Wrap-rollback per scenario so it can be re-run safely.
- scripts/test_wallet_link_security.mjs — added 11 new assertions for
  the consumption-ledger invariants (table existence, unique indexes,
  backfill preservation, FIFO distribution, unlink doesn't touch the
  ledger, CONSUMPTION_DISTRIBUTION_FAILED exception).
- scripts/test_wallet_link_migration.py — mirrors the same static
  checks via Python unittest (25 → 34 tests, all pass).

TOTAL TEST COUNT
----------------
- Node: 58 tests (47 original + 11 new accounting invariants) — all pass
- Python migration static: 34 tests — all pass
- Python accounting scenarios: 6 tests (S1-S5 + cross-identity hijack) — all pass
- Live SQL scenarios: scripts/test_wallet_link_accounting_scenarios.sql
  (run against a configured Supabase instance)
